  "common_questions": [
    {"q": "What is Omnemo OS?", "a": "A friendlier desktop layer for Omarchy: a bottom bar with a taskbar, plain-words menus and a ? guide, floating windows, and Omnemo memory so your AI helpers remember you. It installs on top of stock Omarchy 4.5 and uninstalls cleanly."},
    {"q": "Do I install it instead of Omarchy?", "a": "No. Install Omarchy from its own installer first, then add Omnemo OS with three lines in a terminal."},
    {"q": "Which Omarchy version do I need?", "a": "4.5 stable or newer (`pacman -Q omarchy` shows 4.5.0-1 or later). On a 4.5 test version add --allow-rc. Older versions are refused before anything changes."},
    {"q": "Is it safe? Can I undo it?", "a": "It never uses sudo, refuses if anything it would create is already there, backs up every file it touches before changing it, and `omnemo-os uninstall` puts them all back byte for byte. The README also has you take an Omarchy snapshot first."},
    {"q": "Will it delete my memories?", "a": "No. Uninstall asks \"Keep your memories?\" and Enter keeps them; deleting needs the typed word DELETE and saves a copy to ~/omnemo-memories-<date>.tar.gz first. An Omnemo that was installed before Omnemo OS is never touched."},
    {"q": "Why do my windows float now?", "a": "The edition switches workspaces to floating windows you drag with the mouse. Super + L changes the current workspace's layout; keep pressing until the note says the layout you want."},
    {"q": "An Omarchy update reset something. What now?", "a": "Run `omnemo-os status` to see what, then `omnemo-os repair`. If the bar is one plain colour with no flag, run `omarchy restart shell`."},
    {"q": "How do I update it?", "a": "`omnemo-os update`. It asks nothing, keeps your memories and settings, and never goes backwards. From 0.9.2 or earlier (no update command): clone the repo again and run `bin/omnemo-os update` from the new copy."},
    {"q": "Does it install Claude or Codex for me?", "a": "No. It connects the memory to AI helpers already installed and downloads none. Install a helper later, then run `omnemo-os repair`."},
    {"q": "How do I stop the flag moving?", "a": "In ~/.config/omarchy/shell.toml find `# motion         = \"off\"` and delete the #. It already holds still on battery and on computers without a graphics card."},
    {"q": "Anything to check before my next Omarchy update?", "a": "Run `omnemo-os status` once. If it prints a line starting \"BEFORE your next Omarchy update\", run the command it shows (`sudo pacman -D --asexplicit omarchy`) first. That guards against a stock Omarchy 4.5.0 update issue (omarchy#18154)."},
    {"q": "Can I put my desktop back after an update from 0.9.2?", "a": "Mostly. Uninstall restores everything the edition manages, but what 0.9.2 itself changed at install (the AI helpers' settings files, and Claude Code and Codex downloads of about 840 MB) stays, because safety copies start with 0.9.3."}
  ],

  "why": "Omarchy is fast and beautiful but built for keyboard people. Omnemo OS keeps Omarchy underneath and adds what most people expect: a taskbar, buttons with words on them, a guide to every icon, and windows you can drag. It also gives the AI helpers on your computer one shared memory, so they remember you. One command takes it all back off.",

  "install_steps": [
    {"step": 1, "instruction": "Make sure your computer runs Omarchy 4.5 or newer."},
    {"step": 2, "instruction": "Open a Terminal (Super + Enter) and type `omarchy snapshot create`. It asks for your password. This is your undo point."},
    {"step": 3, "instruction": "Paste these three lines: `git clone https://github.com/GuyMannDude/omnemo-os`, then `cd omnemo-os`, then `bin/omnemo-os install`. It says what it will download before it changes anything."},
    {"step": 4, "instruction": "Wait until it prints that it is installed. The memory step can sit quiet for several minutes on a slow line."},
    {"step": 5, "instruction": "Start a NEW session of your AI helper. It now shares one memory with the others."}
  ],

